  "prompt": "You are a renowned cognitive scientist analyzing computational models of the two-step decision task (Daw et al.). Below is a Python cognitive model that was fit to participant 0's behavior.\n\n```python\ndef cognitive_model1(action_1, state, action_2, reward, model_parameters):\n    \"\"\"\n    Model with separate learning rates for positive and negative RPEs, \n    a single inverse temperature for both stages, model-based weight, \n    eligibility trace, stage 1 and stage 2 stickiness, and decay rate.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    alpha_pos: [0, 1] - Learning rate for positive RPEs\n    alpha_neg: [0, 1] - Learning rate for negative RPEs\n    beta: [0, 10] - Inverse temperature for both stages\n    w: [0, 1] - Model-based weight\n    lam: [0, 1] - Eligibility trace\n    stickiness_1: [-5, 5] - Choice stickiness for stage 1\n    stickiness_2: [-5, 5] - Choice stickiness for stage 2\n    decay_rate: [0, 1] - Forgetting rate for unchosen Q-values (decay towards 0.5)\n    \"\"\"\n    alpha_pos, alpha_neg, beta, w, lam, stickiness_1, stickiness_2, decay_rate = model_parameters\n    n_trials = len(action_1)\n  \n    transition_matrix = np.array([[0.7, 0.3], [0.3, 0.7]])\n    p_choice_1 = np.zeros(n_trials)\n    p_choice_2 = np.zeros(n_trials)\n\n    q_stage1_mf = np.ones(2) * 0.5\n    q_stage2_mf = np.ones((2, 2)) * 0.5\n    last_action_1 = -1\n    last_action_2 = -1\n\n    for trial in range(n_trials):\n        if action_1[trial] == -1 or action_2[trial] == -1 or state[trial] == -1:\n            p_choice_1[trial] = 1.0\n            p_choice_2[trial] = 1.0\n            continue\n            \n        a1 = action_1[trial]\n        s = state[trial]\n        a2 = action_2[trial]\n        r = reward[trial]\n\n        max_q_stage2 = np.max(q_stage2_mf, axis=1)\n        q_stage1_mb = transition_matrix @ max_q_stage2\n        \n        q_net = w * q_stage1_mb + (1 - w) * q_stage1_mf\n        rep_1 = np.zeros(2)\n        if last_action_1 != -1:\n            rep_1[last_action_1] = 1.0\n            \n        logits_1 = beta * q_net + stickiness_1 * rep_1\n        logits_1 -= np.max(logits_1)\n        exp_q1 = np.exp(logits_1)\n        probs_1 = exp_q1 / np.sum(exp_q1)\n        p_choice_1[trial] = probs_1[a1]\n\n        rep_2 = np.zeros(2)\n        if last_action_2 != -1:\n            rep_2[last_action_2] = 1.0\n\n        logits_2 = beta * q_stage2_mf[s] + stickiness_2 * rep_2\n        logits_2 -= np.max(logits_2)\n        exp_q2 = np.exp(logits_2)\n        probs_2 = exp_q2 / np.sum(exp_q2)\n        p_choice_2[trial] = probs_2[a2]\n\n        delta_stage1 = q_stage2_mf[s, a2] - q_stage1_mf[a1]\n        alpha_stage1 = alpha_pos if delta_stage1 > 0 else alpha_neg\n        q_stage1_mf[a1] += alpha_stage1 * delta_stage1\n        \n        delta_stage2 = r - q_stage2_mf[s, a2]\n        alpha_stage2 = alpha_pos if delta_stage2 > 0 else alpha_neg\n        q_stage2_mf[s, a2] += alpha_stage2 * delta_stage2\n        q_stage1_mf[a1] += alpha_stage2 * lam * delta_stage2\n\n        q_stage1_mf[1 - a1] += decay_rate * (0.5 - q_stage1_mf[1 - a1])\n        q_stage2_mf[s, 1 - a2] += decay_rate * (0.5 - q_stage2_mf[s, 1 - a2])\n        q_stage2_mf[1 - s, 0] += decay_rate * (0.5 - q_stage2_mf[1 - s, 0])\n        q_stage2_mf[1 - s, 1] += decay_rate * (0.5 - q_stage2_mf[1 - s, 1])\n        \n        last_action_1 = a1\n        last_action_2 = a2\n\n    eps = 1e-10\n    log_loss = -(np.sum(np.log(p_choice_1 + eps)) + np.sum(np.log(p_choice_2 + eps)))\n    return log_loss\n```\n\nList every distinct psychological mechanism in this model. A mechanism is one separable computational assumption (e.g. \"MB/MF mixture weight\", \"choice stickiness\", \"reward-dependent stickiness\", \"separate stage-2 learning rate\", \"value decay/forgetting\", \"eligibility trace\", \"optimistic Q initialization\").\n\nReturn STRICT JSON only (no prose, no markdown fences):\n{\"mechanisms\": [{\"name\": \"short snake_case id\", \"title\": \"human name\", \"description\": \"on